<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>2.13.4 Solving Inequality Problems Using Tables</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00599</md:content-id>
    <md:title>2.13.4 Solving Inequality Problems Using Tables</md:title>
    <md:uuid>3d46394b-6f5f-4fca-a7ce-9a28fbb9fa80</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Activity (20 minutes)</title>
<para>This activity enables students to continue to use the skills from previous lessons involving inequalities and graphing lines. Students have used a table of values previously to graph linear equations. In this lesson, students will use a table of values to graph the boundary line of an inequality by using the related equation. Students will solve inequalities in two variables using tables to graph the solutions.</para>
<para>The questions in this activity are written as a step-by-step guide to help students identify the key points to help them graph the inequality.</para>
<para>You may want to have students work in pairs so they can talk through the steps. You may need to remind students that the related equation of an inequality is the equation formed by replacing the inequality symbol with an equal sign.</para>
<title>Launch</title>
<para>Students will not use technology to solve the problems in this activity. However, you may want to allow them access to check their graphs. Explain that they will now be solving linear inequalities using tables.</para>
<title>Solving Inequality Problems Using Tables</title>
<title>Student Activity</title>
<para><figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/160ea81093c6e634fbecf786af065f92c29236f9"/></media></figure></para>
<para>For questions 1–5, use the following scenario to graph an inequality from a table:</para>
<blockquote>
  <para>Andre is making trail mix with dried fruit and mixed nuts. Dried fruit costs $3 per pound and mixed nuts cost $6 per pound. He wants to spend no more than $54 on the trail mix. The inequality \(y \leq −2x + 18\) represents the number of pounds of dried fruit, \(y\), he can buy for \(x\) pounds of nuts.</para>
</blockquote>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>The related equation \(y = -2x + 18\) represents the boundary line of the inequality. Complete the table of values to find points on the boundary line of the inequality.</item>
</list>
<table class="os-raise-skinnytable">
  <thead>
    <row>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">\(x\)</entry>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">\(y\)</entry>
    </row>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry>0</entry>
      <entry/>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>2</entry>
      <entry/>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>4</entry>
      <entry/>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>6</entry>
      <entry/>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold">Answer:</para>
<table class="os-raise-skinnytable">
  <thead>
    <row>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">\(x\)</entry>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">\(y\)</entry>
    </row>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry>0</entry>
      <entry>18</entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>2</entry>
      <entry>14</entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>4</entry>
      <entry>10</entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>6</entry>
      <entry>6</entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>Use the graphing tool or technology outside the course. Graph the data from your table of values that represents this scenario using the Desmos tool below. (Students were provided access to Desmos.)</item>
</list>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold">Answer:</para>
<para><figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/380534493b88df7a87955c4e3d4c84bb91997ada"/></media></figure></para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item>Is the boundary line for the inequality \(y  \leq −2x + 18\) a solid line or a dashed line? Explain how you know.</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> The line is solid because the inequality  \(\leq\) means the points on the line are included as solutions.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
  <item>Is the point \((0, 0)\) a solution to the inequality?</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> Yes, \(0  \leq −2(0) + 18\), \(0 \leq 18\).</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="5" type="enumerated">
  <item>Use the graph from number 2. Enter the inequality \(y \leq −2x + 18\) to represent the data. Notice the shading for the solution region of this inequality.</item>
</list>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold">Answer:</para>
<para><figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/d5fd2962509e03697ac49887823e1eef32f14a8d"/></media></figure></para>
<para>In problems 6–10, use the data from the table to graph the inequality and write an inequality with two variables.</para>
<table class="os-raise-skinnytable">
  <thead>
    <row>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">\(x\)</entry>
