<para dir="ltr">These are possible representation of where the line could on the graph</para>
<div class="os-raise-d-flex os-raise-justify-content-between">
<para dir="ltr"><emphasis>Less than</emphasis><figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/959d49a42e607f5d7e2e076c5f89673bcd710eee"/></media></figure></para>
<para dir="ltr"><emphasis>Less than or equal to</emphasis><figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/7b1dd5805d6d51369fde32a9f352f8e2cc408fae"/></media></figure></para>
<para dir="ltr"><emphasis>Greater than</emphasis><figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/3212d93bee75021e7319d2b2cb91f6dadb252198"/></media></figure></para>
<para dir="ltr"><emphasis>Greater than or equal to</emphasis><figure><media alt="ea43ef5b5a2f10d594e518d4673515e5c32687a6 (234×306)"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/ea43ef5b5a2f10d594e518d4673515e5c32687a6"/></media></figure><emphasis> </emphasis></para>
<para dir="ltr"><emphasis>Not a solution</emphasis><figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/3397921522ae5ac7d771896770f8a1b877c5388c"/></media></figure></para></div>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="10" type="enumerated">
  <item>Use the graph from number 7. Shade the correct side of the boundary line to represent the inequality \(y &gt; 3x − 2\).</item>
</list>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold">Answer:</para>
<para><figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/d6d3ad58a2957f93891255478c7771f7e04c06ac"/></media></figure></para>

<title>Anticipated Misconceptions</title>
<para>Students may make a table of values that provide solutions to the inequality. Emphasize that the table of values is used to find the boundary line, which requires identifying the related equation. Students may think they must choose a test point that is a solution of the inequality. Tell students they can use any point as a test point as long as it is not on the boundary line. Tell them \((0, 0)\) is often used because substituting \((0, 0)\) into the inequality makes for easy calculations. Tell them if their test point results in a true statement when substituted into the inequality, then the point is part of the solution. So they should shade the side of the line that contains that point. If the test point results in a false statement, shade the side of the line that does not contain the test point.</para>
<title>Activity Synthesis</title>
<para>Select student pairs to share their solutions. Have them show the work as they substitute the \(x\)-values into the related equation, plot the points, draw the line, and shade. At each step, have students explain how they decided whether the line should be dashed or solid. Ask how they chose the test point and ask if anyone else chose a different test point. Ask how they knew which side of the line to shade. Repeat this for the second scenario. Focus discussions on the process for graphing the solutions of the inequality.</para>
<title>2.13.4: Self Check</title>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold os-raise-text-italicize">After the activity, students will answer the following question to check their understanding of the concepts explored in the activity.</para>
